From: Antumbra build <antumbra@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] net: qrtr: require CAP_NET_ADMIN to create AF_QIPCRTR sockets

QMI services exposed over QRTR carry no client authentication: any
process that can open an AF_QIPCRTR socket can ask the modem's DMS
service to go online, start the GNSS engine or read the sensor DSP.
On a privacy-focused device the radio policy is enforced by root-owned
services over this channel, so unprivileged processes must not reach it.

In-kernel QRTR users (ath10k's WLFW client, the QRTR name service,
the protection-domain mapper) create their sockets with kern=1 and are
unaffected. The userspace daemons that need QRTR (rmtfs, tqftpserv,
pd-mapper, qmicli from root-owned units) all run as root.

Carried by the Antumbra project; not upstream.
---
 net/qrtr/af_qrtr.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

--- a/net/qrtr/af_qrtr.c
+++ b/net/qrtr/af_qrtr.c
@@ -6,3 +6,4 @@
 #include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/capability.h>
 #include <linux/netlink.h>
 #include <linux/qrtr.h>
@@ -1266,6 +1267,15 @@ static int qrtr_create(struct net *net, struct socket *sock,
 	if (sock->type != SOCK_DGRAM)
 		return -EPROTOTYPE;
 
+	/*
+	 * Antumbra: QMI services reachable over QRTR (modem operating mode,
+	 * GNSS, sensor DSP) carry no client authentication, so creating an
+	 * AF_QIPCRTR socket is restricted to privileged processes. In-kernel
+	 * users (ath10k, pd-mapper, remoteproc helpers) pass kern=1.
+	 */
+	if (!kern && !ns_capable(net->user_ns, CAP_NET_ADMIN))
+		return -EPERM;
+
 	sk = sk_alloc(net, AF_QIPCRTR, GFP_KERNEL, &qrtr_proto, kern);
 	if (!sk)
 		return -ENOMEM;
